{:edb/db-before-t 887 :edb/db-after-t 888 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534200 50 #inst "2026-10-06T10:13:08.370Z" 13194139534200 true] [17592186092114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281900 13194139534200 true] [17592186092114 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281906 13194139534200 true] [17592186092116 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281918 13194139534200 true] [17592186092117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186281911 13194139534200 true] [17592186092825 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534200 true] [17592186092825 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\tan x =-\\sqrt{3}$ for $- π < x \\le π$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?" 13194139534200 false] [17592186092825 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139534200 true] [17592186092825 1145 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=\\pi +{x}_{1}=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281900 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281901 13194139534200 true] [17592186281900 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281904 13194139534200 true] [17592186281900 1089 "q-321613" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281900 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534200 true] [17592186281900 1100 "Find the solutions **in radians** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{3}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ Round your answers to **one decimal place**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139534200 true] [17592186281900 1113 #uuid "9bb80790-0622-5f51-a71a-ae64b246abab" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281900 1145 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arctan\\,(\\frac{3}{2})=0.982\\,793…≈1.0\n$$\n\nSince $1.0$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.0.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +0.982\\,793…≈4.1.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=1.0$ and $x=4.1.$" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281901 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281902 13194139534200 true] [17592186281901 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281903 13194139534200 true] [17592186281901 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534200 true] [17592186281901 1041 "field-1" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281901 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281902 13194139534200 true] [17592186281901 1110 #uuid "b27a8733-fe30-59bd-895e-988598143499" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281902 1034 "1.0" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281902 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534200 true] [17592186281902 1111 #uuid "c8d4ba09-aeb4-57ea-9d59-dfa5a08cb4c8" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281903 1034 "0.9" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281903 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534200 true] [17592186281903 1111 #uuid "19cae293-67ed-5504-83e8-3498429d4f9d" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281904 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281905 13194139534200 true] [17592186281904 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534200 true] [17592186281904 1041 "field-2" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281904 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281905 13194139534200 true] [17592186281904 1110 #uuid "02a68d44-70a5-54dc-ba29-6324239f4829" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281905 1034 "4.1" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281905 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534200 true] [17592186281905 1111 #uuid "a5599670-9303-5755-962b-8cc7bd758d6d" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281906 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281907 13194139534200 true] [17592186281906 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281909 13194139534200 true] [17592186281906 1089 "q-321728" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281906 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534200 true] [17592186281906 1100 "Find the solutions **in degrees** to the equation $5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ Round your answers to **the nearest integer**.\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281906 1113 #uuid "fa1833a4-57a9-5cb0-a032-e3e5800c4fc4" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281906 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2 & =0 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-0.4)=-21.8014{…}^{∘}≈-{22.80}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $-{22.80}^{∘}$ lies outside the domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate solutions in the domain, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={180}^{∘}+(-{21.80}^{∘})≈{158.20}^{∘},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={360}^{∘}+(-{21.80}^{∘})≈{338.20}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order and rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are $x={158}^{∘}$ and $x={338}^{∘}.$" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281907 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281908 13194139534200 true] [17592186281907 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534200 true] [17592186281907 1041 "field-1" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281907 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281908 13194139534200 true] [17592186281907 1110 #uuid "3e8c1560-6406-56dd-a155-a0967f779be8" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281908 1034 "158" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281908 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534200 true] [17592186281908 1111 #uuid "24ee3171-c990-58c2-86dd-11454e6dc2db" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281909 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281910 13194139534200 true] [17592186281909 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534200 true] [17592186281909 1041 "field-2" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281909 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281910 13194139534200 true] [17592186281909 1110 #uuid "c205d0dc-c206-56fc-9ba2-b0b446754b53" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281910 1034 "338" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281910 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534200 true] [17592186281910 1111 #uuid "77824142-036a-565d-b480-ab9c4e8341dd" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281911 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281912 13194139534200 true] [17592186281911 1089 "q-72833" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281911 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534200 true] [17592186281911 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981396/assets/aae559883c97bbd19321d9baeb26fe15e47cced5b072e12100c42aa579c9d02d.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the line $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.$ Find, in radians, the $x$-coordinate of the point $A.$" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281911 1113 #uuid "5674b166-f728-5c02-8624-680e618d28d9" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281911 1145 "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two functions gives:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\n Now, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the required domain, and there is only one point of intersection, we conclude that the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point $A$ is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139534200 true] [17592186281912 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281913 13194139534200 true] [17592186281912 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281914 13194139534200 true] [17592186281912 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281915 13194139534200 true] [17592186281912 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281916 13194139534200 true] [17592186281912 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281917 13194139534200 true] [17592186281912 1040 #edb